<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Châlus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Châlus is a small town in the Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the Limousin, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. While besieging its castle of Châlus-Chabrol, held for the viscount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> was struck by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a> bolt on 26 March 1199 and died of the wound on 6 April. The castle keep still stands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalus.jpg" alt="Châlus" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fonquebure · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chateau_Chalus_Chabrol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Châlus is a commune in the Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> department in the Nouvelle-Aquitaine region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its name in Occitan is Chasluç. In 1199 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> was besieging Châlus when Pierre Basile wounded him with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a> bolt, and Richard died of the wound.</p><p>Between 1275 and 1280 Géraud de Maumont built a second castle, Châlus Maulmont, in front of Châlus Chabrol. It was badly damaged during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, dismantled in 1790 and then used as a prison. Its tower collapsed on 20 March 1994. In 1917 American troops were stationed at Châlus, and local tradition says that they dynamited the rock associated with Richard the Lionheart to take a piece back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Châlus lies in the Périgord-Limousin Regional Nature Park, created in 1998. It has the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-de-chalus-chabrol">Château de Châlus-Chabrol</a> and the ruined Château de Châlus-Maulmont. Richard's entrails are still preserved in the chapel, and the town has a medieval garden and a museum dedicated to the chestnut. The inhabitants are known as Chalusiens.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a>, later known as Lawrence of Arabia, marked his 20th birthday at the former Grand Hôtel du Midi on 16 August 1908, while tracing the route of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> on a cycling tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to prepare his thesis on the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> on European military architecture. The siege of Châlus appears in novels and in films, among them Robin and Marian of 1976, which opens on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Châlus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The viscounts were middling lords caught between Aquitaine, the Plantagenet kings and the French crown, often clashing with their own townsmen and with English overlords; Richard the Lionheart died in 1199 besieging <b>Châlus</b>, a castle in the viscounty.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-limoges">Viscounty of Limoges</a></li></ul></section>
